Patch 06 -- run the reviewed Wine DirectSound FIR target as native ARM code.

This builds on patches 01-05.  The bridge is disabled by default and enabled
only with MGS2_BOX86_NATIVE_DSOUND_FIR=1.  At startup it runs a bounded FIR
self-test, then publishes a bridge whose wrapper receives Wine's regparm job
pointer from guest EAX.  The 32-bit job layout is copied field-by-field into
native addresses and the convolution is performed in the Box86 process, so it
does not cross a process or audio API boundary.

The companion Wine patch 36 provides a unique noinline/noclone target.  As with
the existing native memmove and AABB experiments, Box86 accepts it only after
an exact readable-byte signature match and caches exactly one guest address.

On-device at a fixed 1416 MHz, the DirectSound mixer thread fell from 12.97% to
7.57% of one core.  Guest-profile dsound samples fell from 717 to 40, the old
FIR block's 647 samples disappeared, and 260 samples resolved directly to the
native kernel.  This proves the layer removal and mixer saving; it does not by
itself establish an FPS gain because the two gameplay windows had different
music/render states.  Manual combat/SFX validation is still required before
production.  Candidate SHA256:
89ca26c512489ed18b3605ca195fa7dd45d3a9f8cc3fefafac8b1ebd9b86a252.

